10. PRIVATE ADMIN DASHBOARD
=========================================================

The admin dashboard is strictly private.

Its route may be /admin.

The route must not be linked from any public-facing page.

Do not display the dashboard in the public navigation, homepage,
footer, sitemap or public content.

The website owner should access it directly using the private URL.

IMPORTANT:
A private URL alone is not authentication.

Implement secure authentication and authorisation.

AUTHENTICATION

Use Supabase Auth.

Do not hard-code administrator passwords into the application.

Do not expose passwords in frontend JavaScript, HTML, source maps,
logs, GitHub or database queries.

Do not provide public administrator registration.

Set up the initial administrator securely through a documented
Supabase Auth process.

Only explicitly authorised administrator accounts may use
administrative functions.

The owner may configure an initial password during secure setup.
The application must never assume a password is configured until
the authentication account has been created.

The application must support password changes and secure sign-out.

Protect administrative routes and operations using appropriate
server-side checks and Supabase permissions.

Unauthenticated users must not be able to view protected dashboard
data or perform administrative actions.

DASHBOARD FEATURES

Create a clean, responsive dashboard with a private sidebar.

Include:

- Overview
- Learning resources
- Articles
- Podcast episodes
- Impact stories
- Media library
- Drafts
- Published content
- Basic editable site content
- Account settings where supported
- Sign out

Provide content-management forms that support creating, editing,
previewing, publishing, unpublishing and deleting content.

Allow the administrator to save unfinished work as a draft.

Use clear status indicators and confirmation dialogs for destructive
actions.

Validate form fields before saving.

Display accurate loading, success, empty and error states.

Never display a success message when an operation has failed.

Do not use hard-coded content arrays as a substitute for real
database operations.

If the database is unavailable, display an appropriate error state.

